# Expectation record for 05_pointers/007_casts_roundtrip.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_casts_roundtrip
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Object-pointer casts round-tripped through a sufficiently wide integer carrier, with restoration verified by pointer equality and every dereference, subscript and pointer difference guarded on that verification, and the implementation-defined conversion recorded
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
RESTORATION IS VERIFIED, NEVER ASSUMED, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS PROGRAM FREE OF
UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR. Converting a pointer to an integer and back is implementation-defined
in both directions, so the reconstructed pointer is NOT known in advance to designate the
original object. The ground for treating it as usable is recorded in impl_defined_notes
below rather than here, because it is the definition the four reference toolchains give
those conversions and not any property of the carrier. After every round-trip the program
compares the reconstructed pointer against the original and prints that comparison, and
every dereference, every subscript and every pointer difference sits inside a branch
guarded by that comparison. On an implementation whose mapping does not restore the
pointer, each guarded line prints `unrestored` in place of a value: the divergence is
reported as a difference in stdout, which is what the oracles compare, rather than
performed as undefined behaviour. Not one line of this program is reached by reasoning from
carrier width to "the round-trip is exact, therefore the dereference is defined"; that
inference does not hold and the guards exist precisely because it does not.
Inside a taken guard the dereference IS defined: C11 6.5.9p6 makes two object pointers
compare equal only when they designate the same object, so `q == p` together with p
designating a live object of the right type establishes that q designates it too.
WIDTH IS A NECESSARY CONDITION AND NOTHING MORE, and two conversions here DO cross a width
boundary, neither of which truncates. On i686 unsigned long long is 8 bytes while unsigned
long and void * are 4, so the bridge back to a pointer, (unsigned long)carrier, narrows 8
bytes to 4, and the widening that produced the carrier crossed the same boundary upward.
Every carrier value in this file is produced by exactly the pattern
(unsigned long long)(unsigned long)(void *)p -- (const void *) in the const case -- and is
never arithmetically modified afterwards, so it is always the zero-extension of an unsigned
long that is exactly pointer width: every bit above pointer width is zero, and the
narrowing therefore discards only zero bits, which is why every pointer-equality line
reports 1. Both conversions are between unsigned types in any case, so C11 6.3.1.3 makes
even a truncating one defined modular reduction and not undefined behaviour. The two width
relations are checked rather than assumed: they are asserted at translation time with
_Static_assert and printed at run time on the first two output lines as
carrier_wide_enough and bridge_is_pointer_width, both 1 on all four targets, so a target
that broke either one would fail to translate rather than silently lose bits. Every other
width crossing in this file is a widening, which cannot lose a value: each pointer
difference is converted to long long before printing, and each char is read through
unsigned char before being widened to int.
The null pointer round-trip is compared twice and NEVER dereferenced, so it needs no guard:
nothing is read through it. Every pointer that is dereferenced designates an element of its
own array object: iarr has 8 elements indexed 0..7 and carr has 16 indexed 0..15, and every
index reached lies inside those ranges, including the guarded q[-1] and q[2] taken from
&iarr[3], which reach iarr[2] and iarr[5]. No pointer one past the end of an object is
formed anywhere in this file, and every pointer difference is taken between two elements of
one array object, established by the same guard. The char pointer formed over an int object
is converted straight back to int * and is never dereferenced, so no object is read or
written through an lvalue of the wrong type and no aliasing rule is engaged. A plain char
lvalue is read only where the object read is itself an element of the char array, and every
such value is converted to unsigned char before printing. The const object cobj is read
through a const-qualified pointer and is never written.
The three printed execution-character-set codes are pinned by _Static_assert rather than
assumed, so a differing charset is a translation failure rather than a silent divergence.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
CLASSIFICATION, recorded here because it is what makes this record's oracles sound.
POINTER-TO-INTEGER AND INTEGER-TO-POINTER CONVERSION IS THE IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED PROPERTY
UNDER TEST, and it is recorded as such rather than argued away. C11 6.3.2.3p6 makes the
result of converting a pointer to an integer type implementation-defined, and undefined
outright if the result cannot be represented in the integer type. C11 6.3.2.3p5 makes the
reverse conversion implementation-defined and states how far that goes: the result "might
not be correctly aligned, might not point to an entity of the referenced type, and might be
a trap representation". C11 Annex J.3.13 lists both directions among the behaviours a
conforming implementation must document and may choose freely. Only the round trip through
void * is guaranteed by the standard, by 6.3.2.3p1, which is why the void_roundtrip block is
the one block in this program that rests on no implementation definition at all; and the
only round trip through an INTEGER that the standard guarantees compares equal is the one
through intptr_t or uintptr_t of C11 7.20.1.4, both optional types that require stdint.h,
which this program may not include. Equality of the reconstructed pointer, and the validity
of dereferencing it, therefore rest on the definition recorded below.
WIDTH IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT, and an earlier version of this record claimed
otherwise. sizeof(void *) is 4 on i686 and 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64. The carrier
is unsigned long long, 8 bytes on all four targets and therefore always wide enough, and
the conversion back to a pointer is bridged through unsigned long, exactly pointer width on
all four targets (8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64; 4 on i686), so no direct cast between
an 8-byte integer and a 4-byte pointer ever occurs. The bridge itself does cross a width
boundary on i686 and cannot lose a value there either, for the provenance reason set out in
ub_notes above. That reasoning rules out TRUNCATION and rules out nothing else. It says
nothing about which mapping the implementation chooses and it does not establish that
converting back yields a pointer to the original object, so it cannot license an unguarded
dereference. uintptr_t would be the natural carrier and is deliberately NOT used: it is an
optional typedef and would require stdint.h, and no header may be named in this area, so
built-in integer types carry the value instead.
THE DEFINITION, PER TARGET AND PER TOOLCHAIN. All four reference toolchains are GCC 13.4.0,
Ubuntu package 13.4.0-4ubuntu1, each identified by its own -dumpmachine: gcc-13 for
x86_64-linux-gnu, i686-linux-gnu-gcc-13 for i686-linux-gnu, aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc-13 for
aarch64-linux-gnu, riscv64-linux-gnu-gcc-13 for riscv64-linux-gnu. The three non-native
targets execute under QEMU user-mode 10.1.0 on Linux 6.12.85+ x86_64. On each of the four
the definition is the same one: converting a pointer to an integer type wide enough to
represent it yields that pointer's address value unchanged, and converting such an integer
back to a pointer yields the pointer designating that same address, correctly aligned for
the referenced type and designating the same object. That is what makes the reconstructed
pointer compare equal to the original and makes dereferencing it defined. The null pointer
participates on the same terms: it converts to 0 and 0 converts back to a null pointer,
which is why the null block may compare its result and must still never dereference it.
THE MEASURED EVIDENCE for that definition, recorded because the definition is measured on
these four toolchains rather than assumed of them. sizeof(unsigned long) equals
sizeof(void *) on all four, so the pointer-width bridge back to a pointer cannot truncate;
the program asserts this on its second output line. sizeof(unsigned long long) is 8 on all
four, so the carrier always holds a pointer; the program asserts this on its first output
line. Both lines print 1 on all four targets. All four targets were measured to use a flat
address model in which the conversion is the identity on the address value, so every _eq
line prints 1 and every guarded line prints its value; that is the recorded expected_stdout.
The toolchain itself treats the whole chain as an identity rather than as an opaque
conversion, and says so: a comparison between a pointer reconstructed through the full chain
and the original is folded to a self-comparison and diagnosed as always true. This program's
own thirty-eight output lines were then verified byte-identical on all four targets at -O0,
-O1 and -O2, twelve configurations, each exiting 0.
WHY NO TARGET RESTRICTION AND NO ORACLE EXCLUSION IS TAKEN. The definition above is the same
on all four targets and the measured results agree with it, so the target list stays complete
and all three oracles stay enabled. The program does not depend on that measurement being
true, which is the other half of the reason the list is not narrowed: on an implementation
with a different mapping the guards divert to `unrestored` and the run reports a stdout
difference instead of executing undefined behaviour. The measurement fixes the golden record;
the guards keep the program valid whether or not the measurement holds. Had the definitions
differed, or had any target reconstructed a pointer that did not compare equal, that
difference would be implementation-defined rather than a compiler defect, and the correct
handling would be to narrow this record's target list or disable oracle (b) for this program
and state the reason in this field: never to leave it to surface as a finding against a
backend that did nothing wrong, and never to drop the conversion from the corpus, which
would remove the feature requirement 2 asks this program to cover. Equally, it must not be
resolved by deleting the dereferences and keeping only the equality lines -- reading the
designated object is what distinguishes a pointer that merely compares equal from one that
is genuinely usable, and that is the coverage this program exists to provide. The precedent
for recording a dependency of this shape rather than removing the construct is the
signed-narrowing case documented in tests/conformance/README.md.
EXECUTION CHARACTER SET. Three printed values are the codes of 'a', 'f' and 'h', whose
numeric values C11 5.2.1 leaves to the implementation. All four targets were measured to
use ASCII (97, 102, 104) and the program pins those three codes with _Static_assert, so a
differing execution character set fails to translate rather than producing numbers an
oracle would have to attribute to a compiler defect.
Neither a pointer nor a carrier value is ever printed; restoration is observed ONLY by
comparing the reconstructed pointer with the original and by reading the object it
designates.
No address or pointer value is printed. Pointer facts appear only as differences,
comparisons and alignment residues, and every pointer difference is cast to long long
before printing, so the printed text is identical whether ptrdiff_t is 32 or 64 bits.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion
specifiers used anywhere in this program are %d, %lld and fixed-precision %.3f, all of
which are target-width independent.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
carrier_wide_enough=1
bridge_is_pointer_width=1
int_roundtrip_eq=1
int_roundtrip_value=7
int_roundtrip_same_object=1
double_roundtrip_eq=1
double_roundtrip_value=2.500
char_roundtrip_eq=1
char_roundtrip_value=97
char_interior_eq=1
char_interior_value=102
char_interior_offset=5
struct_roundtrip_eq=1
struct_roundtrip_values=3 4
const_roundtrip_eq=1
const_roundtrip_value=99
interior_roundtrip_eq=1
interior_value=40
interior_offset=3
interior_step_back=30
interior_step_forward=60
interior_diff_matches=3
null_roundtrip_eq=1
null_roundtrip_is_null=1
void_roundtrip_eq=1
void_roundtrip_value=70
void_roundtrip_offset=6
bytes_roundtrip_eq=1
bytes_roundtrip_value=30
runtime_roundtrip_eq=1
